/* Domain 44 — UX, UI Design & Accessibility (facts as of October 2026) */
BOOTCAMP.phases.push({
id:"p44", num:44, title:"UX, UI Design & Accessibility", short:"UX",
tagline:"Design software people can actually use — researched, measured and accessible to everyone — and prove it with numbers, standards and real users.",
prereq:"Domains 5 (web: semantic HTML, CSS, JavaScript), 20 (software engineering: testing, delivery, code review) and 39 (mobile: Compose, SwiftUI, platform guidelines); Domain 22's statistics carries the A/B tests and funnels of Modules 2 and 6. You study only products you own or have permission to test, and only people who have given informed consent.",
outcomes:[
"Define usability as ISO 9241-11 does — effectiveness, efficiency and satisfaction for specified users, goals and contexts — and turn each component into a number you can measure.",
"Plan and run user research (interviews, surveys, contextual inquiry, card sorting, tree testing, analytics funnels) with informed consent, and synthesize it into personas, jobs to be done and journey maps without fooling yourself.",
"Design interactions from first principles: information architecture, navigation, forms and error messages, Norman's affordances and signifiers, Fitts's and Hick's laws with arithmetic, feedback and microinteractions.",
"Build a visual system — type scale, color with computed contrast ratios, grids, spacing, icons, dark mode, restrained motion — and express it as design tokens shared by Figma and CSS.",
"Make web and mobile products conform to WCAG 2.2 AA with semantic HTML first and ARIA second, verified with axe, Lighthouse, keyboard testing and the NVDA, VoiceOver and TalkBack screen readers.",
"Explain the ADA (Titles II and III), Section 508, EN 301 549 and European Accessibility Act obligations as they stand in October 2026.",
"Run moderated and unmoderated usability tests, score the System Usability Scale, apply Nielsen's heuristics, and write findings engineers fix.",
"Assemble a portfolio case study and a study plan for the Google UX Design Professional Certificate, IAAP CPACC and, later, IAAP WAS."
],
texts:["Don Norman, The Design of Everyday Things, Revised and Expanded Edition (Basic Books, 2013)","Steve Krug, Don't Make Me Think, Revisited (3rd ed., New Riders, 2014) and Rocket Surgery Made Easy (2009)","W3C, Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 with Understanding WCAG 2.2 and the WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices Guide","Jeff Sauro & James R. Lewis, Quantifying the User Experience (2nd ed., Morgan Kaufmann, 2016)"],
modules:[
{id:"p44m1", title:"What UX Is and Why It Is Engineering: Usability, Process and the Cost of Bad Design", minutes:50,
outcomes:["Define usability with ISO 9241-11:2018 and turn effectiveness, efficiency and satisfaction into measurable targets.","Explain the Double Diamond and the design-thinking stages, and say honestly what each is good and bad for.","Analyze real failures (Hawaii's 2018 missile alert, Citibank's 2020 Revlon payment, the USS John S. McCain collision) as interface defects with root causes.","Distinguish the UX roles and describe exactly how an engineer collaborates with each.","Estimate the cost of a usability defect the way you would estimate the cost of a bug."],
body:`<p>What makes software usable, and why should someone headed for penetration testing care? Because the human is part of the system. Every phishing click (Domain 32), every misread firewall rule, every wrong menu choice on an industrial console is a person meeting an interface. A tester who understands why people err writes sharper social-engineering findings and better remediation; an engineer who understands it ships fewer incidents. <strong>UX</strong> (user experience) is the discipline of designing the whole encounter between a person and a product. This domain treats it the way Domain 20 treats software: as engineering, with requirements, measurements and tests.</p>
<h4>Usability, defined so you can measure it</h4>
<p>ISO 9241-11:2018 defines <strong>usability</strong> as "the extent to which a system, product or service can be used by specified users to achieve specified goals with effectiveness, efficiency and satisfaction in a specified context of use." Every phrase is a requirement. <em>Specified users</em>: not "everyone", but splicers and installers with gloves on. <em>Specified goals</em>: log a reel's footage, not "use the app". <em>Context of use</em>: a truck cab at dusk with glare on the screen, not a designer's 27-inch monitor. The three components become numbers:</p>
<table><tr><th>Component</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Typical metric</th><th>Example target</th></tr>
<tr><td>Effectiveness</td><td>Accuracy and completeness of goal achievement</td><td>Task success rate, error rate</td><td>90% complete the entry unaided</td></tr>
<tr><td>Efficiency</td><td>Resources spent (time, effort) for the result</td><td>Time on task, taps, keystrokes</td><td>Median under 90 s</td></tr>
<tr><td>Satisfaction</td><td>Physical, cognitive and emotional response</td><td>System Usability Scale (SUS), single-ease rating</td><td>SUS of 75 or more</td></tr></table>
<p>Its sibling, ISO 9241-210, describes <em>human-centred design</em> as a loop: understand the context, specify requirements, produce designs, evaluate them, repeat. Don Norman popularized "user experience" when he joined Apple in 1993 as its User Experience Architect, precisely because usability of a screen was too narrow: UX includes onboarding, performance, support, error recovery and the paperwork around the product. UI (user interface) is the layer you see; UX is whether the whole thing works for people. Accessibility (Module 5) is usability for disabled people, and it is the part with a legal standard.</p>
<h4>The Double Diamond and design thinking, honestly assessed</h4>
<p>The UK Design Council's <strong>Double Diamond</strong> (2004–05) draws two cycles of divergence and convergence: <em>Discover</em> (go wide: research the problem), <em>Define</em> (narrow to a problem statement backed by evidence), <em>Develop</em> (go wide again: many solutions, prototypes) and <em>Deliver</em> (narrow: test, refine, ship). The first diamond makes sure you solve the right problem; the second makes sure you solve it right. The 2019 "Framework for Innovation" kept the shape and added principles: people first, communicate visually, collaborate, iterate. <strong>Design thinking</strong>, popularized by IDEO and the Stanford d.school, names five stages: empathize, define, ideate, prototype, test. As a shared vocabulary that forces research before building, both are valuable. Their failure mode is theatre: workshops full of sticky notes, no recruiting rigor, no measurement, no follow-through — the critique Pentagram's Natasha Jen made famous in her 2017 talk. The engineer's rule: every diamond must end in a falsifiable artifact, a problem statement with evidence or a prototype with test results. In Domain 20 terms, discovery and delivery run as dual tracks inside the same sprints.</p>
<h4>The cost of bad UX</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Hawaii, 13 January 2018.</strong> A ballistic-missile alert went to every phone in the state; the correction came 38 minutes later. The software listed drill and live alerts as near-identical choices, showed the same confirmation for both, and had no prepared retraction. The FCC also found the operator believed an attack was real — design and process failed together, and neither caught the other. Fixes: a two-person rule for live alerts, visibly different templates, a pre-built "false alarm" message.</li>
<li><strong>Citibank, August 2020.</strong> Meaning to pay about $7.8 million of interest on a Revlon loan, Citi wired roughly $900 million of its own money to the lenders. In the Oracle FLEXCUBE screen, keeping the principal in-house required setting several fields; the operator set one, and two approvers passed it. A federal judge first let lenders keep about $500 million (2021); the Second Circuit reversed in 2022. A screen that makes "pay $7.8 million" and "pay $900 million" look alike is a defect.</li>
<li><strong>USS John S. McCain, August 2017.</strong> Ten sailors died in a collision. Investigators found that confusion over a touchscreen steering and thrust system contributed; in 2019 the Navy decided to return destroyers to physical throttles and helm controls.</li></ul>
<p>Good design pays the other way. Jared Spool's "$300 million button" story: an e-commerce checkout that forced registration lost buyers; replacing the Register button with Continue (registration optional) raised purchases 45% and revenue $300 million in the first year. Do the arithmetic yourself: if 50,000 people a month reach a checkout and a confusing field makes 3% abandon, at a $40 average order that is 1,500 × $40 = $60,000 a month — a number that funds a designer and a research budget.</p>
<h4>Who does what, and where engineers fit</h4>
<table><tr><th>Role</th><th>Owns</th><th>What the engineer brings</th></tr>
<tr><td>UX researcher</td><td>Studies people; plans and runs research; synthesis</td><td>Observes sessions, supplies analytics and logs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Product / interaction designer</td><td>Flows, information architecture, wireframes, prototypes</td><td>Early feasibility review, data and edge cases</td></tr>
<tr><td>Visual / UI designer</td><td>Type, color, layout, components</td><td>Implements with tokens, flags performance cost</td></tr>
<tr><td>Content designer / UX writer</td><td>Words, labels, errors, microcopy</td><td>Real error conditions and their causes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accessibility specialist</td><td>Standards, audits, assistive-technology testing</td><td>Semantic code, acceptance criteria, fixes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Design-system team / UX engineer</td><td>Shared components and design tokens in code</td><td>Adoption, bug reports, contributions</td></tr></table>
<p>Engineers who collaborate well do five things: watch at least one research session per project (one live struggle beats ten reports), review designs before they are final, demand every state (empty, loading, error, offline, very long text), build from the design system rather than raw values, and write accessibility and analytics acceptance criteria into tickets. The security link is direct: <em>usable security</em> is security people can operate. Push-approval prompts that trained people to tap Approve were exploited in MFA-fatigue attacks such as the 2022 Uber breach; number matching is a UX fix. CAPTCHAs that block blind users are a WCAG failure (Module 5).</p>
<h4>How this maps to the certifications</h4>
<p>Course 1 of the Google UX Design Professional Certificate (Foundations of User Experience Design) covers exactly this ground: what UX is, the roles in a design team, the design process and the business value of good design. IAAP's CPACC tests "accessibility, usability and user experience" inside its Accessibility and Universal Design domain (40% of the exam): be able to state the ISO definition, explain why accessibility is a subset of usability with its own standard, and give a cost-of-failure example in one sentence.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Usability as a number: a field crew's as-built entry.</strong> Specified users: fiber installers and splicers, often gloved. Goal: record reel ID, footage in and out, and splice locations before leaving site. Context: a tablet in a truck at dusk. Baseline test with 8 crew members: 6 of 8 finish unaided (75% effectiveness), median 4 min 10 s (efficiency), mean SUS 58 (below the cross-industry average of 68). Observed causes: 32-pixel keypad keys missed with gloves, no barcode scan for the reel label, and the error "Invalid input" with no explanation. Redesign: 64-pixel keys, a scan button, and "Footage out must be larger than footage in (1,020 ft)". Retest: 8 of 8, median 2 min 05 s, SUS 79. Value: 40 crews × 1 entry a day × about 2 minutes saved × 250 working days = 20,000 minutes ≈ 333 hours ≈ $13,300 a year at $40 an hour, before counting the rework that wrong footage used to cause.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"UX is making things pretty.\" Visual design is one layer; UX is whether specified users reach specified goals effectively, efficiently and with satisfaction — a measurable property, like latency.","\"Users are like me.\" You know the system's internal model; they do not. Research the people in the actual context of use — the alert operator, not the developer who wrote the menu.","\"Design thinking is a workshop you run once.\" Its value is evidence at the end of each diamond — a validated problem statement, a tested prototype — repeated every iteration."],
problems:[
{q:"Write an ISO 9241-11 usability requirement for this app's flashcard review: users, goal, context and one target per component.", a:"Self-taught adult learners (users) review 20 due cards (goal) on a phone during a 15-minute work break (context): at least 95% of sessions completed without error; median under 6 s per card; mean SUS of 75 or more (or single-ease rating of 6/7)."},
{q:"A checkout gets 80,000 sessions a month; 1.5% abandon because the postcode field rejects spaces. Average order $55. Upper-bound monthly loss?", a:"80,000 × 0.015 = 1,200 abandoned sessions × $55 = $66,000 a month (an upper bound: not every abandoner would have bought)."},
{q:"Decompose the Hawaii false alert into three design defects and give one fix for each.", a:"(1) Drill and live choices adjacent and similar → separate, visibly different templates; (2) identical confirmation → a confirmation that states 'LIVE alert to every phone in Hawaii' and requires a second person; (3) no recovery path → a pre-built correction message ready to send in seconds."},
{q:"Place each activity on the Double Diamond: interviews with crews; writing 'How might we…' problem statements; paper prototypes; usability test of the coded beta.", a:"Discover; Define; Develop; Deliver."},
{q:"Write the five things you, as the engineer, require on every design before you build it.", a:"All states (empty, loading, error, offline, long text); responsive behavior; design tokens instead of raw values; accessibility notes (headings, focus order, accessible names, roles); the success metric and the analytics events that measure it."}
],
checks:[{q:"ISO 9241-11's three usability components?",a:"Effectiveness, efficiency and satisfaction — for specified users, goals and context of use."},{q:"The four phases of the Double Diamond?",a:"Discover, Define, Develop, Deliver."},{q:"Who popularized 'user experience' and where?",a:"Don Norman, as Apple's User Experience Architect (1993)."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA why usability is an engineering property you can measure, using ISO 9241-11's three components and one real failure (Hawaii 2018 or Citibank 2020) as evidence."},

{id:"p44m2", title:"User Research: Asking, Watching and Measuring Without Fooling Yourself", minutes:60,
outcomes:["Choose a research method from the question you need answered (say vs do, qualitative vs quantitative, generative vs evaluative).","Run interviews and surveys that resist leading questions, social desirability and sampling bias, and size samples with arithmetic.","Conduct contextual inquiry and synthesize it with affinity mapping into personas, jobs to be done and journey maps.","Use card sorting, tree testing and analytics funnels to study structure and behavior (Domain 22 statistics).","Write a research plan with informed consent, privacy and ethics built in."],
body:`<p>What do people actually need, and how do you find out without hearing what you want to hear? User research is the part of UX most like penetration testing: you form hypotheses, gather evidence in a scoped engagement with consent, and report what is true rather than what is convenient. The failure mode is the same as a sloppy pentest too — confirmation bias dressed up as findings.</p>
<h4>Choosing a method</h4>
<p>Christian Rohrer's landscape of methods (Nielsen Norman Group) sorts them on two axes: <em>attitudinal</em> (what people say) versus <em>behavioral</em> (what people do), and <em>qualitative</em> (why and how) versus <em>quantitative</em> (how many, how much). A third distinction is timing: <em>generative</em> research discovers problems; <em>evaluative</em> research tests a solution. People are poor predictors of their own behavior, so when the question is about behavior, watch behavior.</p>
<table><tr><th>Method</th><th>Say / do</th><th>Qual / quant</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
<tr><td>Interviews</td><td>Say</td><td>Qual</td><td>Goals, motivations, past episodes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Surveys</td><td>Say</td><td>Quant</td><td>How common an attitude or behavior is</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contextual inquiry</td><td>Do</td><td>Qual</td><td>Real workflows, workarounds, environment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Card sorting</td><td>Say</td><td>Both</td><td>How people group content (generative)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tree testing</td><td>Do</td><td>Quant</td><td>Whether a hierarchy is findable (evaluative)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Analytics, funnels</td><td>Do</td><td>Quant</td><td>Where and how many — never why</td></tr>
<tr><td>A/B test (Domain 22)</td><td>Do</td><td>Quant</td><td>Which design causes a better outcome</td></tr></table>
<h4>Interviews and surveys, and their biases</h4>
<p>Interviews are semi-structured: a guide of open prompts, followed wherever the participant leads. Ask about specific past behavior ("Tell me about the last time you logged a reel"), never hypotheticals ("Would you use voice entry?"), and never pitch. Rob Fitzpatrick's <em>The Mom Test</em> compresses the craft: talk about their life, not your idea; ask for specifics in the past; talk less. Plan 5–8 participants per user segment; Guest, Bunce and Johnson (2006) found most themes appear within 12 interviews and the core ones within 6. The biases to design out: <em>leading questions</em> and <em>confirmation bias</em> (yours), <em>social desirability</em> and <em>acquiescence</em> (theirs: saying the nice or agreeable thing), and the <em>observer effect</em> (people behave differently when watched).</p>
<p>Surveys measure prevalence, not causes. Their own biases: <em>sampling bias</em> (who you can reach), <em>non-response bias</em> (who bothers to answer), <em>double-barreled</em> items ("Is the app fast and easy?"), leading wording and order effects. The arithmetic for a proportion at 95% confidence:</p>
<pre>margin of error ≈ 1.96 × sqrt( p(1 − p) / n ),  worst case p = 0.5
n = 100   → ±9.8 points
n = 400   → ±4.9 points
n = 1,000 → ±3.1 points</pre>
<p>That margin covers random sampling error only. A survey answered only by power users is biased at any size; quadrupling n halves the margin and fixes none of the bias.</p>
<h4>Contextual inquiry and synthesis</h4>
<p><strong>Contextual inquiry</strong> (Beyer and Holtzblatt, <em>Contextual Design</em>, 1998) means going where the work happens and adopting a master–apprentice relationship: the participant works, you watch and ask, and you check your interpretations with them on the spot. Collect artifacts and photos with permission: the clipboard of paper as-built sheets, the sticky note on a monitor, the workaround spreadsheet — every workaround is an unmet requirement. Synthesis turns notes into insight. <strong>Affinity mapping</strong> (the KJ method, after Jiro Kawakita) puts one observation per note, clusters bottom-up, names the clusters and states insights. Three deliverables follow:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Personas</strong> (Alan Cooper, 1999): archetypes defined by goals, behaviors and context, grounded in research. A "proto-persona" written from assumptions must be labeled as one.</li>
<li><strong>Jobs to be done</strong> (Christensen, Ulwick, Moesta): people "hire" a product to make progress in a circumstance. A job story reads: "When I finish a splice enclosure at dusk, I want to log footage in under a minute, so I can leave the site before dark."</li>
<li><strong>Journey maps</strong>: stages across the top; actions, thoughts, emotions, pain points and opportunities down the side, for one persona in one scenario. A service blueprint adds the backstage processes and systems.</li></ul>
<h4>Structure and behavior: card sorting, tree testing, analytics</h4>
<p>In a <strong>card sort</strong>, participants group topics into categories: <em>open</em> (they create and name groups), <em>closed</em> (predefined groups) or <em>hybrid</em>. Analysis uses a similarity matrix (the share of participants who put two cards together) and a dendrogram from hierarchical clustering. Nielsen (2004) found about 15 participants reach a 0.90 correlation with large samples; Tullis and Wood (2004) recommend 20–30 for stable results. <strong>Tree testing</strong> is the reverse: a text-only hierarchy and tasks such as "Where would you find the WAS exam fee?", measuring success, directness (no backtracking), time and first click; aim for 50 or more participants when you need tight estimates. Tools: Optimal Workshop's OptimalSort and Treejack, Maze, UXtweak — or index cards on a table.</p>
<p>Analytics shows behavior at scale. A <strong>funnel</strong> computes conversion at each step: 20,000 sessions → 6,000 start the form (30%) → 2,400 reach payment (40%) → 1,800 complete (75%), 9% overall. The biggest leak is the first step, and analytics cannot tell you why — pair the drop with session observation or a usability test. Google's HEART framework (Happiness, Engagement, Adoption, Retention, Task success), each with goals, signals and metrics, keeps dashboards tied to user outcomes instead of vanity counts; remember Goodhart's law, because a metric that becomes a target stops measuring.</p>
<h4>Ethics and consent</h4>
<p>The Belmont Report (1979) still frames the duty: <em>respect for persons</em> (<strong>informed consent</strong>), <em>beneficence</em> (minimize harm) and <em>justice</em> (fair selection of who bears the burden). In practice: a plain-language consent form stating the purpose, what is recorded, who will see it, how long it is kept and that the participant may stop at any time and keep the incentive; separate consent for video and for using clips; no recording of customers' addresses or other bystanders; recordings encrypted and deleted on schedule; parental consent for minors; and at work, participation that is voluntary and never reported to managers. Recordings are personal data under GDPR (Domain 33), so you need a lawful basis, minimization and retention limits. Academic studies require an ethics board (IRB); industry research does not, but the principles bind you anyway — the same way written authorization binds a penetration test.</p>
<h4>How this maps to the certifications</h4>
<p>Google's Course 2 (Start the UX Design Process: empathize, define, ideate) builds personas, user stories, journey maps and problem statements; Course 4 (Conduct UX Research and Test Early Concepts) covers research plans, consent, usability studies and affinity diagrams. CPACC's first domain (Disabilities, Challenges and Assistive Technologies, 40%) is the reason to recruit disabled participants in every round, not as a separate study at the end.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>A research plan: "Logging as-builts before dark."</strong> <em>Background:</em> crews log footage on paper and re-key it later; 1 entry in 12 needs correction. <em>Research questions:</em> (1) What happens between the last splice and leaving site? (2) Where do footage errors originate? (3) What would make same-day digital logging the easy path? <em>Methods:</em> 6 contextual inquiries (two installers, two splicers, two leads, at real job sites), a 12-question survey of 120 technicians (margin ≈ 1.96 × √(0.25/120) = ±8.9 points), and a funnel of the existing web form (started 1,000 → submitted 610). <em>Recruiting screener:</em> at least 3 months in the field; include at least one participant with a color-vision deficiency or dyslexia. <em>Consent:</em> audio and screen recording, no customer addresses on camera, 90-day retention, $50 gift card kept even if they stop. <em>Analysis:</em> affinity map within 48 hours of the last visit. <em>Deliverables:</em> a journey map, three job stories, the top five problems with severity, and a list of assumptions that research disproved.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Just ask users what they want.\" People report wants poorly and predict their own behavior worse; ask about specific past episodes and watch them work.","\"A survey of 1,000 people is unbiased because it is big.\" Sample size shrinks random error only; a biased sample stays biased at any n.","\"Personas are demographic profiles.\" A persona is a research-grounded archetype of goals, behaviors and context; age and a stock photo predict nothing."],
problems:[
{q:"Rewrite three biased interview questions: 'Wouldn't a barcode scanner make this easier?', 'How much do you love the new form?', 'Would you use voice entry?'", a:"'Walk me through how you recorded the last reel.' / 'Tell me about the last time the form gave you trouble.' / 'Tell me about a time you had to enter data with your hands busy — what did you do?'"},
{q:"Margin of error at 95% confidence for n = 250, worst case?", a:"1.96 × √(0.25/250) = 1.96 × 0.0316 ≈ ±6.2 percentage points."},
{q:"Funnel: 12,000 visits → 3,600 start → 1,080 reach payment → 810 complete. Step rates, overall rate, and what you investigate first, with which method?", a:"30%, 30%, 75%; overall 6.75%. Investigate the first two steps (largest absolute and relative loss) with usability tests or session observation, since analytics shows where but not why."},
{q:"In a card sort with 20 participants, 17 grouped 'CPACC fee' with 'Exam format' and 3 with 'Pricing'. Similarity, and what should a tree test verify?", a:"85% similarity with Exam format. A tree test should check that ≥ 80% of participants find the fee under the proposed Exams branch, directly and on the first click."},
{q:"Outline a consent form for recording field technicians with a head-mounted camera.", a:"Purpose; what is recorded (audio, video, screen); exclusion of customers, addresses and plates; voluntary participation and the right to stop with the incentive kept; storage encrypted, retained 90 days; who sees raw footage; separate opt-in for clips in reports; contact for withdrawal of data."}
],
checks:[{q:"Attitudinal vs behavioral research?",a:"What people say vs what people do."},{q:"Open vs closed card sort?",a:"Participants create and name their own groups vs sort into predefined categories."},{q:"The three Belmont principles?",a:"Respect for persons (informed consent), beneficence, justice."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA how you would find out why fiber crews avoid a logging app: pick methods from the say-versus-do and qualitative-versus-quantitative axes, size the survey, and describe how you obtain informed consent."},

{id:"p44m3", title:"Interaction Design and Prototyping: Structure, Laws, Forms, Figma and Handoff", minutes:60,
outcomes:["Organize content with information architecture (organization, labeling, navigation, search) and choose a navigation pattern.","Apply Norman's affordances, signifiers, mapping, feedback and the gulfs of execution and evaluation; tell slips from mistakes.","Compute Fitts's and Hick's laws and use them to size, place and order controls.","Design forms whose labels, validation timing and error messages meet WCAG.","Build a Figma prototype with components, variants and auto layout, and hand it off with design tokens mapped to CSS custom properties."],
body:`<p>How do you make the next action obvious? Interaction design is the logic layer of UX: what a person can do, in what order, with what feedback. Like a protocol in Domain 2, an interface is a set of states and transitions, and like a protocol it fails at the edges — the timeout, the malformed input, the interrupted flow.</p>
<h4>Information architecture and navigation</h4>
<p><strong>Information architecture</strong> (Rosenfeld, Morville and Arango) has four systems: organization (how content is grouped), labeling (what things are called), navigation (how you move) and search. Build it bottom-up from a card sort, then verify it with tree testing before anyone draws a screen. Navigation must answer three questions on every screen: Where am I? Where can I go? How do I get back?</p>
<table><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Use when</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr>
<tr><td>Tab bar / navigation bar</td><td>3–5 top destinations on mobile</td><td>More than five crowds labels</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drawer ("hamburger")</td><td>Many secondary destinations</td><td>Hidden navigation is discovered and used less</td></tr>
<tr><td>Top navigation, mega menu</td><td>Desktop sites with broad sections</td><td>Hover-only menus fail keyboard and touch</td></tr>
<tr><td>Breadcrumbs</td><td>Deep hierarchies</td><td>Not a replacement for primary navigation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Facets and filters</td><td>Large catalogs</td><td>Show result counts; avoid zero-result filters</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stepper / wizard</td><td>Linear tasks with dependencies</td><td>Let people go back without losing data</td></tr></table>
<p>This app's bottom rail (Base, Path, Recall, Labs, Class, ADA) is a tab bar with six items — one over the platform guidance — saved by visible text labels under every icon.</p>
<h4>Norman's vocabulary</h4>
<p>An <strong>affordance</strong> is the relationship between an object's properties and a person's capabilities that determines what actions are possible: a button affords pressing, a handle affords pulling. A <strong>signifier</strong> (the term Norman added in the 2013 edition) is any perceivable signal that shows where to act and how: a label, a raised edge, an underline, a cursor change. Designers cannot paint affordances onto glass; they design signifiers, and much "flat design" trouble is missing signifiers. Add <em>mapping</em> (the layout of controls matches their effects, like stove knobs arranged like the burners), <em>constraints</em> (physical, logical, cultural), <em>feedback</em> (immediate and informative) and a coherent <em>conceptual model</em>. Two gulfs separate a person from a system: the <em>gulf of execution</em> (how do I do this?) is bridged by signifiers and mapping; the <em>gulf of evaluation</em> (did it work?) is bridged by feedback. Errors come in two kinds. A <em>slip</em> is the right goal with the wrong action — tapping Delete next to Save; fix it with spacing, constraints and undo. A <em>mistake</em> is the wrong goal or plan — deleting a record you wrongly believed was a duplicate; fix it with a better conceptual model and confirmations that state consequences.</p>
<h4>The laws, with arithmetic</h4>
<p><strong>Fitts's law</strong> (1954; the Shannon form by MacKenzie, 1992) predicts pointing time from distance D and target width W: the index of difficulty is a number of <em>bits</em>.</p>
<pre>MT = a + b × log2(D / W + 1)
D = 600 px, W = 24 px:  ID = log2(26)   = 4.70 bits
D = 600 px, W = 48 px:  ID = log2(13.5) = 3.75 bits
with a = 0.10 s, b = 0.15 s/bit:  0.81 s vs 0.66 s per acquisition</pre>
<p>Doubling the width saves about 0.14 s on every tap — multiplied by every tap of every user. Consequences: make frequent targets large, keep related controls close, put destructive ones far from common ones. Screen edges are "infinitely wide" targets for a mouse (the macOS menu bar) but not for a finger. Minimums: WCAG 2.2's Target Size (Minimum) is 24 × 24 CSS pixels (AA) and 44 × 44 at AAA; Apple asks for 44 × 44 points and Material for 48 × 48 dp.</p>
<p><strong>Hick's law</strong> (Hick 1952, Hyman 1953) models choice time among familiar, equally likely options: RT = a + b × log2(n + 1). Eight options cost log2(9) = 3.17 bits, sixteen cost log2(17) = 4.09 bits, so doubling a known menu adds under one bit. It does not apply to unfamiliar lists, where people scan item by item and time grows with n; there grouping and labels matter more than count. And Miller's "7 ± 2" is about short-term memory, not menu length. Response-time limits complete the picture: about 0.1 s feels instant, 1 s keeps the flow of thought, 10 s loses attention — so show progress beyond a second and percent-done beyond ten. Domain 5's performance work is UX work.</p>
<h4>Forms, validation and error messages</h4>
<p>Forms are where users pay you in effort. Use one column; visible labels above fields (a placeholder is not a label); <code>fieldset</code> and <code>legend</code> for groups; the right input types (<code>type="email"</code>, <code>inputmode="numeric"</code>) and <code>autocomplete</code> tokens such as <code>postal-code</code>, which WCAG 1.3.5 requires for personal data; accept the formats people type, such as spaces in card numbers. Validate when a person leaves a field, not on every keystroke, and re-check live once an error is showing; always validate again on the server (Domain 20). Luke Wroblewski's 2009 study with Etre found inline validation raised successful completion 22% and cut completion time 42%. An error message says what happened and how to fix it, next to the field, in text rather than color alone (WCAG 1.4.1, 3.3.1, 3.3.3); on submit, show an error summary at the top that links to each field and receives focus, the GOV.UK pattern. "Invalid input" fails every one of those tests; "Enter footage out as a number larger than footage in (1,020 ft)" passes.</p>
<pre>&lt;label for="fout"&gt;Footage out (ft)&lt;/label&gt;
&lt;input id="fout" name="fout" inputmode="numeric"
       aria-invalid="true" aria-describedby="fout-err"&gt;
&lt;p id="fout-err"&gt;Footage out must be larger than footage in (1,020 ft).&lt;/p&gt;</pre>
<h4>Feedback and microinteractions</h4>
<p>Dan Saffer's <em>Microinteractions</em> (2013) decomposes every small moment — a toggle, a like, pull-to-refresh, "Copied" — into a trigger, rules, feedback, and loops and modes. <strong>Microinteractions</strong> are where perceived quality lives: acknowledge input within 100 ms, use skeleton screens for loads longer than a second, prefer undo to confirmation dialogs for reversible actions (Gmail's Undo Send), and use optimistic UI only when you can roll back honestly with a clear message. Status changes must reach screen-reader users too (WCAG 4.1.3: <code>role="status"</code>).</p>
<h4>Prototyping in Figma, and the handoff</h4>
<p>Fidelity climbs from sketch to <strong>wireframe</strong> (structure only), mockup (visual) and prototype (clickable); test at the lowest fidelity that answers the question. In Figma, a <em>component</em> is a main definition with instances that update when it changes; <em>variants</em> group versions of one component by properties (state = default, hover, focus, disabled); component properties expose text, booleans and instance swaps; <em>auto layout</em> is Domain 5's CSS flexbox in a design tool (direction, gap, padding, hug, fill, fixed, wrap); <em>variables</em> store colors, numbers and strings with modes such as light and dark; prototyping wires frames together with interactions and smart animate; Dev Mode gives engineers inspectable specs. The <strong>handoff</strong> is a conversation, not an export: every state, edge cases (long names, empty data, errors, offline), responsive rules, accessibility annotations (headings, landmarks, focus order, accessible names) and <strong>design tokens</strong> — design decisions stored as named data in tiers: primitive (<code>cyan-400</code> = #00e5ff), semantic (<code>color-action</code>) and component (<code>button-primary-bg</code>). The W3C Design Tokens Community Group format is JSON with <code>$type</code> and <code>$value</code>; tools such as Style Dictionary compile it to CSS custom properties, Android resources and Swift.</p>
<pre>{ "color": {
    "cyan-400": { "$type": "color", "$value": "#00e5ff" },
    "action":   { "$type": "color", "$value": "{color.cyan-400}" } } }
/* compiled */
:root { --color-cyan-400: #00e5ff; --color-action: var(--color-cyan-400); }</pre>
<p>This app already does the CSS half: its <code>:root</code> declares <code>--cyan</code>, <code>--mag</code> and <code>--bg</code>, and every figure in this domain draws with those variables.</p>
<h4>How this maps to the certifications</h4>
<p>Google's Course 3 (Build Wireframes and Low-Fidelity Prototypes), Course 5 (Create High-Fidelity Designs and Prototypes in Figma) and Course 6 (Build Dynamic User Interfaces for Websites: information architecture and sitemaps) are this module. Target size, labels, error identification and status messages are WCAG criteria that WAS tests directly and CPACC tests conceptually.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>A gloved keypad and a splice-type menu, by the numbers.</strong> The footage keypad has 32-pixel keys and an average reach of 120 px: ID = log2(120/32 + 1) = log2(4.75) = 2.25 bits. Enlarging keys to 64 px gives log2(120/64 + 1) = log2(2.875) = 1.52 bits; with gloves, b ≈ 0.2 s/bit, so each press saves about 0.15 s — and, more valuable, misses drop sharply because the target is twice the fingertip error. The splice-type picker lists 14 familiar options. Splitting it into a first menu of 4 categories and a second of about 4 items looks simpler but costs 2 × log2(5) = 4.64 bits plus a second movement, against log2(15) = 3.91 bits for one flat list. Decision: one flat list ordered by frequency with the four most common pinned at the top, 48-px rows, and an Undo for the last entry instead of a confirmation dialog.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Fewer choices is always faster.\" Hick's law covers familiar, equally likely options; splitting a known 14-item list into two levels adds a second decision and a second movement — broad, well-labeled menus often win.","\"Placeholder text is a fine label.\" It disappears on input, usually has poor contrast and is a last-resort accessible name; use a visible label element.","\"Handoff means exporting the mockups.\" Engineers need states, edge cases, responsive rules, accessibility annotations and tokens; a static mockup shows one happy path."],
problems:[
{q:"Fitts's law: D = 400 px with W = 40 px vs W = 20 px; a = 0.1 s, b = 0.15 s/bit. Compute ID and MT for both.", a:"log2(11) = 3.46 bits → 0.62 s; log2(21) = 4.39 bits → 0.76 s."},
{q:"A familiar menu grows from 6 to 12 equally likely items; b = 0.2 s/bit. Added decision time by Hick's law?", a:"log2(7) = 2.81 → log2(13) = 3.70 bits: +0.89 bits × 0.2 ≈ +0.18 s."},
{q:"Rewrite 'Error 422: invalid field' for a date of birth entered as 31/02/1990, and say where it appears.", a:"'Enter a real date — February has 28 or 29 days.' Shown next to the field (linked by aria-describedby, field marked aria-invalid) and in a focused error summary that links to the field."},
{q:"Classify and fix: (a) a user meant Save but tapped the adjacent Delete; (b) a user deleted a reel record believing it was a duplicate.", a:"(a) Slip: separate and shrink the destructive action, add spacing, offer undo. (b) Mistake: show what makes records duplicates, confirm with specifics ('Reel R-1182, 1,250 ft, logged today'), use soft delete."},
{q:"Write a three-tier token chain for this app's primary button background and the CSS it compiles to.", a:"Primitive cyan-400 = #00e5ff → semantic color-action = {cyan-400} → component button-primary-bg = {color-action}; CSS: --color-cyan-400:#00e5ff; --color-action:var(--color-cyan-400); --button-primary-bg:var(--color-action)."}
],
checks:[{q:"Affordance vs signifier?",a:"What an object makes possible vs the perceivable signal that shows where and how to act."},{q:"Fitts's law formula (Shannon form)?",a:"MT = a + b × log2(D/W + 1)."},{q:"Which Figma feature behaves like CSS flexbox?",a:"Auto layout."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA how you would size and arrange the controls of a field-data form using Fitts's and Hick's laws, then how you would hand it to an engineer with tokens and every state."},

{id:"p44m4", title:"Visual Design Fundamentals: Type, Color, Layout, Systems — and a Critique of This App", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Build a modular type scale with readable line length, line height and hierarchy.","Compute WCAG contrast ratios from hex colors and design palettes that survive color-vision deficiency and dark mode.","Lay out screens on grids with an 8-point spacing system and consistent iconography.","Use motion with restraint and honor prefers-reduced-motion.","Compare Material Design 3, Apple's Human Interface Guidelines and a custom design system, and critique this app's cyberpunk theme with measured numbers."],
body:`<p>Why does one screen feel clear and another feel like noise when both hold the same content? Visual design is information design: it decides what the eye sees first, what belongs together, and whether the text can be read at all. Little of it is taste alone; most of it is perception science and arithmetic you can check.</p>
<h4>Typography</h4>
<p>Body text on the web starts at 16 px (the browser default) and 17–18 px for long reading. Line height of 1.4–1.6 suits body text, and WCAG 1.4.12 (Text Spacing) requires that nothing breaks when a user forces 1.5× line height, 2× paragraph spacing, 0.12 em letter spacing and 0.16 em word spacing. <strong>Line length</strong> of 45–75 characters, about 66 ideal (Bringhurst), keeps the eye's return sweep reliable; set it in CSS with <code>max-width</code> in <code>ch</code>. A <strong>type scale</strong> multiplies a base by one ratio: 16 × 1.25<sup>n</sup> gives 16, 20, 25, 31.25 and 39 px (the "major third"); 1.333 gives 16, 21.3, 28.4, 37.9 and 50.5. Four to six steps, used consistently, beat a dozen ad-hoc sizes. Build hierarchy with two or three of size, weight, color, spacing and position — not all five at once. Use a workhorse sans (Inter, Roboto, SF Pro) for interface text, keep display faces for headings, and reserve all caps for short labels with a little extra tracking, because all-caps paragraphs slow reading.</p>
<h4>Color and contrast, computed</h4>
<p>WCAG defines a <strong>contrast ratio</strong> from relative luminance. Take each sRGB channel c from 0–255, divide by 255, linearize it, weight the channels by how sensitive the eye is to each, and compare:</p>
<pre>c' = c/255;  lin = c' ≤ 0.04045 ? c'/12.92 : ((c' + 0.055)/1.055)^2.4
L  = 0.2126 R + 0.7152 G + 0.0722 B
ratio = (L_lighter + 0.05) / (L_darker + 0.05)        range 1:1 … 21:1
#767676 on white: 118/255 = 0.4627 → lin 0.181 → (1.05)/(0.231) = 4.54:1</pre>
<p>That gray is the lightest that passes AA for normal text. The thresholds: normal text needs 4.5:1 for AA and 7:1 for AAA; large text (at least 24 px, or 18.66 px bold) needs 3:1 and 4.5:1; component boundaries, focus indicators and meaningful chart lines need 3:1 (1.4.11 Non-text Contrast); logos and disabled controls are exempt. About 8% of men and 0.5% of women of Northern European ancestry have a red–green color-vision deficiency, so meaning never rides on hue alone (1.4.1 Use of Color): add text, icons, shape or pattern, check designs with Chrome DevTools' vision-deficiency emulation, and use a colorblind-safe palette such as Okabe–Ito for Domain 22's charts. HSL is not perceptually uniform; OKLCH in CSS Color 4 is, which makes even tonal ramps (Material's 0–100 tones) easy to build.</p>
<h4>Layout, grids, spacing and icons</h4>
<p>Gestalt principles explain grouping: proximity (the strongest cue), similarity, common region, continuity, closure, figure–ground. Spacing usually beats boxes and lines for showing what belongs together. Grids give columns, gutters and margins — commonly 4 columns on phones, 8 on tablets, 12 on desktop — and Domain 5's CSS Grid implements them directly. An 8-point spacing system (4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32, 48, 64) makes every gap a deliberate step, with space inside a group smaller than the space between groups. Icons share one grid (24 × 24 with a 2 px stroke is common) and one style, outlined or filled, never mixed. Only a few icons are universally understood (search, home, close), so most need a visible label; icon-only buttons need an accessible name such as <code>aria-label</code>, and decorative icons get <code>aria-hidden="true"</code>.</p>
<h4>Dark mode and motion</h4>
<p><strong>Dark mode</strong> is a second design, not an inversion. Use dark gray surfaces (Material's baseline is #121212) rather than pure black, which causes halation for many readers; off-white text on #121212 still reaches about 17:1. Desaturate bright accents, which vibrate on dark grounds; show elevation with lighter surfaces instead of shadows; re-check every ratio, because mid-grays that passed on white often fail on black; honor <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> and offer a toggle. Motion should orient, give feedback or show continuity — never decorate. Most interface transitions sit at 100–300 ms with ease-out for entering elements. Parallax and zoom effects can trigger vestibular symptoms, so honor <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> (technique C39 for WCAG 2.3.3); anything that moves, blinks or scrolls on its own for more than five seconds needs a pause, stop or hide control (2.2.2, Level A); nothing may flash more than three times a second (2.3.1).</p>
<h4>Design systems</h4>
<p>A <strong>design system</strong> is tokens plus components plus patterns plus guidelines plus governance — with code. Material Design 3 (Google) brought dynamic color and tonal palettes, refreshed in 2025 as Material 3 Expressive; Apple's Human Interface Guidelines define platform conventions, Dynamic Type and 44-point targets, and the Liquid Glass material introduced in 2025 drew legibility complaints during its betas — a live lesson in style against contrast. Study IBM Carbon, Microsoft Fluent 2 and the GOV.UK Design System, whose patterns are tested with disabled users. To start your own, begin with tokens and the ten components you actually use, document each with states and accessibility notes, and version it like a library (Domain 20's semantic versioning).</p>
<h4>A measured critique of this app's cyberpunk theme</h4>
<p>Strengths first. Lecture text (#e6f6ff on #060a14) measures 17.9:1 and body text 17.1:1, both AAA; the secondary gray <code>--dim</code> reaches 7.7:1; the cyan, magenta and amber accents reach 12.9, 6.2 and 13.7:1. Lectures are set in Inter at 18 px, line height 1.75, capped at 68ch — textbook readability. Focus shows a 2 px cyan outline with a 2 px offset (12.9:1), animation sits behind <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>, and figures draw with tokens and carry text labels. The problems are specific. The <code>--faint</code> token (#4d6f8c) measures 3.74:1 on the page and labels 10–11 px monospace text, failing 1.4.3; #5f84a3 keeps the hue and reaches 5.0:1 on the page and 4.6:1 on panels. Input and answer borders use <code>--line</code> (#15385e) at 1.66:1, failing the 3:1 that 1.4.11 asks of component boundaries; #4a78a3 reaches 3.9–4.3:1. Quiz feedback signals right and wrong with green and red alone (1.4.1). The scanline overlay lays a 35%-opacity texture over all text, lowering effective contrast for low-vision readers, and 10 px uppercase labels with 0.14–0.22 em tracking are hard to read at any contrast. Verdict: the aesthetic is legitimate — keep the style in the chrome and the clarity in the content; changing two tokens and two patterns reaches AA without losing the look.</p>
<h4>How this maps to the certifications</h4>
<p>Google's Course 5 covers typography, color, iconography, design systems and accessibility in visual design; CPACC's Accessibility and Universal Design domain tests WCAG's color and contrast principles; WAS tests the exact criteria — 1.4.1, 1.4.3, 1.4.10, 1.4.11 and 1.4.12 — at the level of measuring and remediating them.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>A brand color you can defend.</strong> The app's darker cyan, #0097b3, is chosen for a light-theme button with white text. Luminance of #0097b3 = 0.254; white = 1.0; ratio = 1.05 / 0.304 = 3.46:1 — enough for large text (3:1) but not for a 16 px label (4.5:1). Darkening to #007a91 (L = 0.160) gives 1.05 / 0.210 = 5.01:1, passing AA for normal text while staying recognizably cyan. On the dark theme the same token family flips: cyan #00e5ff behind #060a14 text gives 12.9:1. Encode both as semantic tokens — <code>color-action-bg</code> = #007a91 in light mode and #00e5ff in dark mode, <code>color-on-action</code> = #ffffff and #060a14 — so components never hard-code a hex value and every pairing was computed once, by script, before anyone shipped it.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Pure white on pure black is the most readable dark mode.\" It maximizes contrast at 21:1 but causes halation for many readers, especially with astigmatism; off-white on dark gray (around 15–17:1) reads better.","\"If it passes 4.5:1 it is readable.\" Contrast is necessary, not sufficient: 10 px text, wide tracking, thin weights and busy textures can make passing text hard to read.","\"Red and green status colors are fine; everyone knows them.\" About 1 in 12 men cannot reliably tell them apart; pair color with text, icons or shape."],
problems:[
{q:"Compute the contrast of white on #0097b3 (luminance 0.254) and say what text sizes it supports.", a:"(1 + 0.05)/(0.254 + 0.05) = 3.46:1 — passes AA only for large text (≥ 24 px or ≥ 18.66 px bold) and for non-text components; fails normal text."},
{q:"Build a five-step type scale from 16 px at ratio 1.333.", a:"16, 21.3, 28.4, 37.9, 50.5 px."},
{q:"Average character width of 18 px Inter is about 0.5 em. What maximum width keeps lines near 66 characters?", a:"66 × 9 px ≈ 594 px; in CSS, max-width of roughly 66ch (ch is the width of '0', so check visually)."},
{q:"The --line border (#15385e) fails 1.4.11 against #060a14 and #0a1626. State the requirement and verify a fix.", a:"Component boundaries need 3:1 against adjacent colors. #4a78a3 measures 4.25:1 on #060a14 and 3.90:1 on #0a1626 — passes."},
{q:"A dashboard chart uses only a red and a green line. Give two fixes.", a:"Direct-label each line and give them different dash patterns or markers; switch to a colorblind-safe pair (Okabe–Ito orange and blue) with 3:1 against the background."}
],
checks:[{q:"WCAG AA contrast for normal text, large text and UI components?",a:"4.5:1, 3:1 and 3:1."},{q:"Recommended line length for body text?",a:"About 45–75 characters, around 66 ideal."},{q:"CSS media query for users who want less motion?",a:"prefers-reduced-motion."}],
explain:"Walk ADA through computing a contrast ratio from two hex colors, then explain which of this app's tokens fail WCAG AA and the smallest fixes that keep the cyberpunk look."},

{id:"p44m5", title:"Accessibility by Law and by Craft: WCAG 2.2, ARIA, Assistive Technology and the CPACC Body of Knowledge", minutes:60,
outcomes:["Explain WCAG 2.2's structure (POUR, 13 guidelines, 86 success criteria, Levels A/AA/AAA) and apply its key AA criteria.","Use semantic HTML first and ARIA only where HTML falls short, following the first rule of ARIA, and work out accessible names.","Make interfaces keyboard-operable with visible, unobscured focus, and test them with NVDA, VoiceOver and TalkBack.","Provide captions, transcripts and audio description, and design for cognitive accessibility.","State the ADA, Section 508, EN 301 549 and European Accessibility Act obligations as of October 2026 and map them to the CPACC body of knowledge."],
body:`<p>Can everyone use what you built — someone blind, deaf, one-handed, dyslexic, with a tremor, or reading in direct sunlight? The World Health Organization counts about 1.3 billion people, one in six worldwide, living with significant disability, and everyone has temporary or situational limits: a broken wrist, a loud room, gloves in January. Accessibility is the part of UX with a legal standard, a test method and lawsuits. It is also the part engineers most often get wrong, because the failures are invisible to anyone who does not use assistive technology.</p>
<h4>WCAG 2.2: how the standard is built</h4>
<p><strong>WCAG</strong> 2.2 became a W3C Recommendation on 5 October 2023 and was approved as the international standard ISO/IEC 40500:2025 in October 2025. It rests on four principles, <strong>POUR</strong>: Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, Robust. Under them sit 13 guidelines and 86 testable success criteria at three levels: A (31), AA (24 more) and AAA (31). A page conforms at AA only when it meets all 55 A and AA criteria; AA is the legal target almost everywhere, and AAA is not required as a whole of any site. Version 2.2 added nine criteria and removed 4.1.1 Parsing as obsolete. WCAG 3 is a Working Draft (September 2026) and years from completion; W3C's own advice is to meet 2.2 now.</p>
<table><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Level</th><th>Plain meaning</th><th>Typical failure</th></tr>
<tr><td>1.1.1 Non-text Content</td><td>A</td><td>Text alternatives for images and icons</td><td>Missing alt, or alt="image"</td></tr>
<tr><td>1.2.2 Captions (Prerecorded)</td><td>A</td><td>Synchronized captions for video</td><td>Unedited auto-captions</td></tr>
<tr><td>1.3.1 Info and Relationships</td><td>A</td><td>Structure in code: headings, lists, labels</td><td>Bold text styled to look like a heading</td></tr>
<tr><td>1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum)</td><td>AA</td><td>4.5:1 text, 3:1 large text</td><td>Pale gray body text</td></tr>
<tr><td>1.4.10 Reflow</td><td>AA</td><td>No two-way scrolling at 320 CSS px</td><td>Fixed-width layouts</td></tr>
<tr><td>2.1.1 Keyboard</td><td>A</td><td>Everything works from a keyboard</td><td>A div with only an onclick</td></tr>
<tr><td>2.4.7 Focus Visible</td><td>AA</td><td>You can see where focus is</td><td>outline: none with no replacement</td></tr>
<tr><td>2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Min.)</td><td>AA, new</td><td>Focused item not fully hidden</td><td>Sticky banner covers the focused link</td></tr>
<tr><td>2.5.7 Dragging Movements</td><td>AA, new</td><td>Single-pointer alternative to dragging</td><td>Drag-only reordering</td></tr>
<tr><td>2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum)</td><td>AA, new</td><td>24 × 24 CSS px, or enough spacing</td><td>Tiny icon buttons packed together</td></tr>
<tr><td>3.3.7 Redundant Entry</td><td>A, new</td><td>Don't make people re-type data</td><td>Re-entering the same address</td></tr>
<tr><td>3.3.8 Accessible Authentication (Min.)</td><td>AA, new</td><td>No cognitive test to log in without an alternative</td><td>Paste blocked in password fields; puzzle CAPTCHAs</td></tr>
<tr><td>4.1.2 Name, Role, Value</td><td>A</td><td>Controls expose name, role and state</td><td>Custom toggle with no role or state</td></tr>
<tr><td>4.1.3 Status Messages</td><td>AA</td><td>Status announced without moving focus</td><td>A silent "Saved" toast</td></tr></table>
<h4>Semantic HTML first, ARIA second</h4>
<p>The browser builds an <em>accessibility tree</em> from the DOM: every node has a role, an <strong>accessible name</strong>, and states and properties. A <strong>screen reader</strong> reads that tree, not your pixels — Domain 5's DOM seen by a different client. The first rule of <strong>ARIA</strong> (W3C, "Using ARIA"): if a native HTML element or attribute already has the semantics and behavior you need, use it instead of repurposing another element and adding ARIA. A real button is focusable, fires on Enter and Space, and announces "button" with its text as the name, for free; a div needs a role, a tabindex and key handlers to imitate it, and usually ends up imitating it badly. WebAIM's annual Million report has found, year after year, more detected errors on home pages that use ARIA than on pages that don't. The accessible name comes, in order of precedence, from <code>aria-labelledby</code>, then <code>aria-label</code>, then native labeling (a label element, alt, legend, caption), then element content for buttons and links, with title and placeholder as weak last resorts.</p>
<pre>&lt;!-- inaccessible --&gt;  &lt;div class="btn" onclick="save()"&gt;Save&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;!-- accessible   --&gt;  &lt;button type="button" onclick="save()"&gt;Save&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;!-- icon only    --&gt;  &lt;button type="button" aria-label="Close dialog"&gt;
                          &lt;svg aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"&gt;…&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;!-- status       --&gt;  &lt;div role="status"&gt;Settings saved&lt;/div&gt;</pre>
<p>ARIA earns its place for patterns HTML lacks: tabs, comboboxes, disclosure widgets (<code>aria-expanded</code>), live regions (<code>role="status"</code>, <code>aria-live="polite"</code>). The native <code>dialog</code> element opened with <code>showModal()</code> now supplies focus containment and Esc. For each pattern, follow the keyboard model in the WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices Guide. Landmarks (header, nav, main, footer) and a logical heading outline are how screen-reader users skim; most navigate by headings first.</p>
<h4>Keyboard, focus and screen readers</h4>
<p>Tab and Shift+Tab move between interactive elements; Enter activates links and buttons; Space activates buttons and checkboxes; arrow keys move inside composite widgets (tabs, menus, radio groups, using a roving tabindex); Esc closes popups and dialogs. Focus order must follow reading order (2.4.3), so never use a positive tabindex. In a single-page app, move focus to the new view's heading on every route change and update the document title; when a dialog closes, return focus to the button that opened it. Provide a skip link to the main content (2.4.1).</p>
<table><tr><th>Screen reader</th><th>Platform</th><th>Commands to learn first</th></tr>
<tr><td>NVDA (free, NV Access)</td><td>Windows</td><td>NVDA key = Insert or Caps Lock; H next heading; D next landmark; NVDA+F7 elements list; NVDA+Space browse/focus mode</td></tr>
<tr><td>JAWS (commercial)</td><td>Windows</td><td>Most-used desktop reader in WebAIM's 2024 survey, NVDA close behind</td></tr>
<tr><td>VoiceOver</td><td>macOS</td><td>Cmd+F5 on/off; VO = Control+Option; VO+Right next item; VO+U rotor</td></tr>
<tr><td>VoiceOver</td><td>iOS, iPadOS</td><td>Swipe right/left next/previous; double-tap activates; two-finger rotate for the rotor</td></tr>
<tr><td>TalkBack</td><td>Android</td><td>Swipe right/left; double-tap activates; TalkBack menu for reading controls</td></tr></table>
<p>Test the pairings people actually use: NVDA with Firefox or Chrome, JAWS with Chrome, VoiceOver with Safari, TalkBack with Chrome — the Compose and SwiftUI semantics you met in Domain 39 are what TalkBack and VoiceOver read on native apps.</p>
<h4>Media, cognition and the rest</h4>
<p>Prerecorded video needs captions (A) and audio description of important visual content (AA); live video needs captions (AA); audio-only content needs a transcript. Automatic captions are a draft: edit them, because uncorrected errors fail the criterion; live events use CART or professional captioning. <em>Cognitive accessibility</em> — W3C's guidance "Making Content Usable for People with Cognitive and Learning Disabilities" — asks for clear purpose, plain language, consistent navigation (3.2.3) and help (3.2.6), forgiving inputs, adjustable time limits (2.2.1) and no memory or puzzle tests to log in (3.3.8). The security link: allowing password managers and paste is both an accessibility requirement and better security, and it helps the next phishing-resistant login design you review.</p>
<h4>The law, as of October 2026</h4>
<ul><li><strong>ADA</strong> (1990), Title III (businesses open to the public): no web standard in regulation, but the Department of Justice and most courts treat such websites as covered — <em>Robles v. Domino's</em> (9th Cir. 2019; the Supreme Court declined review) — and thousands of suits are filed a year, measured against WCAG AA. Title II (state and local government): the DOJ's April 2024 rule adopted WCAG 2.1 AA for websites and mobile apps; an interim final rule of 20 April 2026 moved compliance to 26 April 2027 for entities serving 50,000 or more people and 26 April 2028 for smaller ones and special districts.</li>
<li><strong>Section 508</strong> of the Rehabilitation Act binds federal agencies' technology; its 2017 refresh (effective January 2018) incorporates WCAG 2.0 A and AA, and vendors prove conformance with an Accessibility Conformance Report built on the VPAT template.</li>
<li><strong>European Accessibility Act</strong> (Directive (EU) 2019/882) has applied since 28 June 2025 to new products and services offered to EU consumers, wherever the seller is based: computers and smartphones, ATMs and ticket machines, e-commerce, banking, e-books, passenger-transport services and electronic communications. Microenterprises providing services (fewer than 10 staff and no more than €2 million turnover or balance sheet) are exempt; a transition runs to 28 June 2030 for existing contracts and products already in use, and self-service terminals may serve up to 20 years.</li>
<li><strong>EN 301 549</strong> is the European harmonised standard. Version 3.2.1 (WCAG 2.1 AA) is still the one cited in the Official Journal; version 4.1.1, aligned with WCAG 2.2 AA, was published by ETSI in September 2026 and becomes the presumption-of-conformity reference once cited.</li></ul>
<p>Overlay widgets that promise one-line compliance do not repair inaccessible code; in 2025 the FTC ordered one vendor, accessiBe, to pay $1 million over such claims.</p>
<h4>The CPACC body of knowledge</h4>
<p>IAAP's CPACC has three domains. <em>Disabilities, Challenges and Assistive Technologies</em> (40%): the medical, social and biopsychosocial models of disability (the WHO's ICF), categories of disability and the barriers each meets, assistive technologies (screen readers, magnifiers, braille displays, switch access, eye tracking, speech recognition), demographics and etiquette. <em>Accessibility and Universal Design</em> (40%): individual accommodation versus universal design, the seven principles of universal design (equitable use, flexibility, simple and intuitive use, perceptible information, tolerance for error, low physical effort, size and space for approach and use), Universal Design for Learning, WCAG, and the curb-cut effect. <em>Standards, Laws and Management Strategies</em> (20%): the UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (2006), national and procurement laws such as the ADA, Section 508, Ontario's AODA, the UK Equality Act 2010 and the EAA, and organizational governance. The current CPACC body of knowledge cites WCAG 2.1; the WAS body of knowledge (v2.3, October 2024) uses WCAG 2.2 and WAI-ARIA 1.2.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Fixing a custom toggle and a silent toast.</strong> Before: <code>&lt;div class="toggle on" onclick="flip()"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code> and a toast that fades in "Saved". Audit: not focusable (2.1.1), no name, role or state (4.1.2), on and off shown only by color (1.4.1), toast never announced (4.1.3). After: <code>&lt;button type="button" aria-pressed="true"&gt;Dark mode&lt;/button&gt;</code> styled as a switch with an on/off icon, plus <code>&lt;div role="status"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code> whose text is set to "Settings saved". Verification: Tab reaches the control with a visible ring; Space flips it; NVDA reads "Dark mode, toggle button, pressed", then "Settings saved" without moving focus; VoiceOver on iOS reads the same after a swipe; <code>npx @axe-core/cli</code> reports no violations for it. Eight minutes of work removed four failures across two Level A criteria and two AA criteria.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Adding ARIA makes it accessible.\" ARIA only changes what the accessibility tree reports; it adds no keyboard behavior, and wrong ARIA is worse than none — use native elements first.","\"An overlay widget makes us compliant.\" Overlays cannot fix inaccessible source, and the FTC penalized a vendor in 2025 for claiming they could; conformance comes from the code.","\"Accessibility is for blind users.\" It spans vision, hearing, motor, cognitive, speech and situational limits; captions, target size, plain language and keyboard access serve millions who never touch a screen reader."],
problems:[
{q:"A page meets every Level A criterion and every AA criterion except 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast. What level does it conform to?", a:"Level A only; AA conformance requires all A and all AA criteria (55 in WCAG 2.2)."},
{q:"Work out the accessible names: (a) a button with aria-label='Close' containing a span 'X'; (b) a label 'Email' for an input with placeholder 'you@example.com'.", a:"(a) 'Close' — aria-label overrides the content. (b) 'Email' — the label element wins; the placeholder is only a last-resort fallback."},
{q:"Name the failing criterion: (a) a sticky cookie banner hides the focused link; (b) the login blocks paste into the password field; (c) 16 × 16 px icon buttons with no spacing.", a:"(a) 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum); (b) 3.3.8 Accessible Authentication (Minimum); (c) 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum)."},
{q:"A US county of 80,000 people asks when its website must meet the Title II rule. Answer with the standard and the date.", a:"WCAG 2.1 AA by 26 April 2027, after the DOJ's 20 April 2026 interim final rule extended the original 24 April 2026 date."},
{q:"A 25-person US startup sells online to customers in France. Does the European Accessibility Act apply, and what standard gives presumption of conformity today?", a:"Yes: e-commerce to EU consumers is in scope and 25 staff is not a microenterprise. EN 301 549 v3.2.1 (WCAG 2.1 AA) until v4.1.1 is cited in the Official Journal — build to WCAG 2.2 AA so you are ready for both."}
],
checks:[{q:"What does POUR stand for?",a:"Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, Robust."},{q:"WCAG 2.2 AA minimum target size?",a:"24 × 24 CSS pixels, or enough spacing around smaller targets."},{q:"The first rule of ARIA?",a:"If a native HTML element or attribute has the semantics and behavior you need, use it instead of adding ARIA to something else."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA the first rule of ARIA with a button example, then name the laws that would apply to a US company selling online in the EU and the WCAG level each points to."},

{id:"p44m6", title:"Usability Testing and Measurement: Tasks, SUS, Heuristics, Experiments and Audits", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Plan moderated and unmoderated usability tests with realistic tasks, success criteria and think-aloud.","Score the System Usability Scale and compute confidence intervals for small-sample task success.","Run a heuristic evaluation with Nielsen's ten heuristics and severity ratings.","Design an A/B test with a sample-size estimate (Domain 22) and know when not to run one.","Audit accessibility with axe, Lighthouse and manual assistive-technology testing, and write findings engineers fix."],
body:`<p>Does it work for people — and how sure are you? Usability testing is to design what Domain 20's test suite is to code: it replaces opinion with evidence. You will measure it the way you measured loss budgets and latency, with numbers and error bars.</p>
<h4>Planning a test</h4>
<p><em>Moderated</em> tests put a facilitator with the participant, in person or remotely: rich data, follow-up questions, five to eight people a round, and the risk of the facilitator steering. <em>Unmoderated</em> tests (Maze, UserTesting, Lookback) scale cheaply but cannot probe, so tasks must be crystal clear and you recruit 20 or more when you want metrics. <em>Formative</em> tests find problems; <em>summative</em> tests measure against a benchmark. Nielsen and Landauer (1993) modeled the share of problems found as 1 − (1 − L)<sup>n</sup>; with an average problem affecting L = 31% of users, five users find 1 − 0.69<sup>5</sup> ≈ 84%. The fine print matters: that holds for one homogeneous user group, and a problem affecting only 10% of users is caught by five people just 1 − 0.9<sup>5</sup> = 41% of the time — so test in several small rounds, and per segment.</p>
<p>A task is a realistic goal without interface words: "Find out what the CPACC exam costs for a non-member," not "Click Certifications." Define the success criteria before the session, counterbalance task order, and pilot the script once. In a <strong>think-aloud</strong> session the participant narrates while working; the facilitator prompts neutrally ("What are you thinking?", "What would you expect to happen?") and never helps. Use a retrospective think-aloud when time on task matters. Record success (complete, partial, fail), time, errors and the 7-point Single Ease Question after each task, and the System Usability Scale at the end — with informed consent, as in Module 2, and pay people for their time.</p>
<h4>SUS and small-sample statistics</h4>
<p>The <strong>System Usability Scale</strong> (John Brooke; built at Digital Equipment in 1986, published 1996) is ten statements rated 1–5. Odd items are positive and even items negative, so they are scored differently:</p>
<pre>odd items (1,3,5,7,9):   score = response − 1
even items (2,4,6,8,10): score = 5 − response
SUS = (sum of the ten scores) × 2.5             range 0–100
responses 4 2 5 1 4 2 5 2 4 2
odd  4,5,4,5,4 → 3,4,3,4,3 = 17
even 2,1,2,2,2 → 3,4,3,3,3 = 16      (17 + 16) × 2.5 = 82.5</pre>
<p>SUS is not a percentage. Across hundreds of studies the average is about 68 (Sauro); around 80 puts a product in the top tenth, and scores near 50 sit in the bottom sixth. Report a mean with a confidence interval, not a lone number. Small samples are humbling: with 4 of 5 participants succeeding, the adjusted-Wald 95% interval for the true success rate runs from about 36% to 98%. Five users find problems; they do not measure rates. Task times are right-skewed, so report the median or geometric mean.</p>
<h4>Heuristic evaluation</h4>
<p>A <strong>heuristic evaluation</strong> has three to five evaluators inspect an interface independently against Nielsen's ten usability heuristics (1994, refined from Nielsen and Molich, 1990), then merge findings. Nielsen found that a single evaluator catches about 35% of problems and five catch about 75%. The ten: visibility of system status; match between the system and the real world; user control and freedom; consistency and standards; error prevention; recognition rather than recall; flexibility and efficiency of use; aesthetic and minimalist design; help users recognize, diagnose and recover from errors; help and documentation. Rate severity 0–4 (0 not a problem, 1 cosmetic, 2 minor, 3 major, 4 catastrophe) from frequency, impact and persistence. A cognitive walkthrough complements it for first-time use, asking at each step whether a newcomer would know what to do, notice the control and understand the feedback. Both are cheap and fast; neither replaces watching real users.</p>
<h4>Experiments and analytics (Domain 22)</h4>
<p>An <strong>A/B test</strong> randomizes users between designs and compares one primary metric fixed in advance, with a sample size set by the minimum detectable effect. Lehr's rule approximates it for 80% power at α = 0.05: n ≈ 16 p(1 − p) / δ² per arm. A 10% baseline completion rate and a target lift of 2 points needs 16 × 0.09 / 0.0004 = 3,600 users per arm. Do not peek and stop early; watch guardrail metrics (errors, support contacts, accessibility). An experiment tells you which design wins, never why, and it needs traffic most small products lack — there, a five-person test is the better instrument. A design that "wins" by trickery is still a dark pattern: the FTC's 2022 report documented them, and the EU Digital Services Act bans deceptive interfaces on online platforms.</p>
<h4>Accessibility audits</h4>
<p>Automated tools are fast and partial. axe-core (Deque) powers axe DevTools and Lighthouse's accessibility score; WAVE (WebAIM), Microsoft's Accessibility Insights and Pa11y cover similar ground. Deque's 2021 study found automated rules caught about 57% of issues by volume; the UK government's 2017 test of tools against 142 planted barriers found the best caught about 40%. A Lighthouse score of 100 is not conformance.</p>
<pre>npx @axe-core/cli http://localhost:5173 --tags wcag2a,wcag2aa,wcag21aa,wcag22aa
npx lighthouse http://localhost:5173 --only-categories=accessibility --view
npx pa11y --standard WCAG2AA http://localhost:5173</pre>
<p>The manual half: a keyboard-only pass (reach everything, visible focus, no traps, logical order); a <strong>screen reader</strong> pass (headings list, landmarks, labels, button names, announced status); zoom to 200% and reflow at 320 px; forced text spacing; color-only meaning; captions; motion. W3C's evaluation methodology, WCAG-EM 2.0 (published as a Note in July 2026), tells you how to sample pages for a site-wide verdict. Structurally an audit is a pentest report: scope, method, evidence, severity, remediation, retest.</p>
<h4>Writing findings engineers act on</h4>
<p>Every finding gets a title stating user impact, evidence (screenshot, clip timestamp, axe rule ID, WCAG criterion), who is affected and how often, a severity, a specific recommendation, and testable acceptance criteria. A real one from this app: <em>Finding 3 — severity 3, WCAG 2.1.1 (A): key-term definitions cannot be opened from the keyboard.</em> Evidence: Tab reaches a highlighted term and shows the focus ring, but Enter and Space do nothing, and the popup closes when a mouse moves onto it (1.4.13). Fix: render each term as a button with <code>aria-expanded</code> and <code>aria-controls</code>, open on Enter or Space, close on Esc, keep it open while hovered. Acceptance: a keyboard-only tester opens and closes three definitions, and NVDA announces "button, collapsed" and "expanded". Prioritize by severity × frequency, file tickets, and retest after the fix — accessibility regressions return like any other <strong>regression</strong>.</p>
<h4>How this maps to the certifications</h4>
<p>Google's Course 4 runs a full usability study (plan, conduct, synthesize, present) and Course 5 tests the high-fidelity prototype. WAS's second domain, Identifying Accessibility Issues (40%), is the audit craft above: testing tools, assistive-technology testing and conformance judgment; its third, Remediating (20%), is severity, prioritization and recommending fixes.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Five-user test of this app's Recall deck.</strong> Five learners (consented, $30 each) do five tasks with think-aloud. SUS scores: 82.5, 70, 57.5, 77.5 and 65 → mean 70.5; standard deviation 9.9; 95% interval = 70.5 ± 2.776 × 9.9/√5 = 58 to 83. Honest summary: "slightly above the 68 average, with an interval spanning below average to excellent." Task results matter more: Task 2 ("look up the meaning of a highlighted term without using a mouse") succeeded 1 of 5 times (adjusted-Wald interval 2% to 64%) — the keyboard defect in Finding 3. Task 4 ("tell whether your last quiz answer was right") took a median of 9 s because feedback was color-only; two participants guessed. Ranked fixes: keyboard-operable term buttons (severity 3), text-plus-icon quiz feedback announced with role="status" (severity 3), a visible 'cards due' count on the Recall tab (severity 2). Retest next round with five new people.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Five users is enough for any study.\" Five per round finds most common problems in one user group; measuring rates, comparing designs or covering several segments needs many more.","\"A SUS score of 70 means the product is 70% usable.\" SUS is not a percentage; 70 is just above the cross-industry average of about 68.","\"Lighthouse gives us 100, so we are accessible.\" Automated rules catch roughly 40–57% of barriers; keyboard and screen-reader testing find the rest."],
problems:[
{q:"Score SUS responses 3,3,4,2,3,3,4,2,3,3.", a:"Odd 3,4,3,4,3 → 2,3,2,3,2 = 12; even 3,2,3,2,3 → 2,3,2,3,2 = 12; (12 + 12) × 2.5 = 60."},
{q:"With L = 0.31, what share of problems do 3 users find? 8 users?", a:"1 − 0.69³ ≈ 67%; 1 − 0.69⁸ ≈ 95%."},
{q:"Baseline sign-up is 20%; you want to detect a lift to 22%. Per-arm sample size by Lehr's rule?", a:"16 × 0.2 × 0.8 / 0.02² = 2.56 / 0.0004 = 6,400 users per arm."},
{q:"Name the heuristic violated: (a) no indicator while a 20-second lab check runs; (b) 'Recall' used as the only name for the flashcard deck; (c) delete with no undo.", a:"(a) Visibility of system status; (b) match between the system and the real world; (c) user control and freedom (and error prevention)."},
{q:"Rewrite 'Accessibility bad on quiz page' as an actionable finding.", a:"Title: right/wrong feedback is conveyed by color only and is silent to screen readers. Criteria 1.4.1 (A), 4.1.3 (AA). Evidence: screenshot plus NVDA log. Affected: colorblind and screen-reader users, every question. Severity 3. Fix: icon plus text 'Correct' / 'Incorrect — the answer is B' in a role='status' region. Acceptance: grayscale screenshot still shows the result; NVDA announces it without focus moving."}
],
checks:[{q:"SUS scoring rule?",a:"Odd items: response − 1; even items: 5 − response; sum × 2.5."},{q:"Average SUS score across studies?",a:"About 68."},{q:"Nielsen's severity scale?",a:"0–4: not a problem, cosmetic, minor, major, catastrophe."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA how to score SUS, why five users can find problems but cannot measure success rates (use the 4-of-5 confidence interval), and how you would turn one observation into a ticket."},

{id:"p44m7", title:"Portfolio, Process and the Exams: Case Studies, Collaboration, Content Design and Careers", minutes:50,
outcomes:["Write a portfolio case study that shows problem, process, evidence, decisions and measured outcomes.","Work with product and engineering through critique, design reviews, design QA and DesignOps.","Write microcopy and content with content-design principles and plain language.","Plan the Google UX Design certificate's project sequence and a CPACC-then-WAS exam strategy with real dates and fees.","Compare UX and accessibility career paths, including security-adjacent ones, with realistic pay."],
body:`<p>How do you prove you can do this work to someone who has never met you? In UX the portfolio is the credential: certificates open the door, case studies get the offer. Your combination is unusual and valuable — years in harsh field contexts, engineering depth from the earlier domains, and a security mindset. Accessibility teams and usable-security teams struggle to hire exactly that mix.</p>
<h4>The case study</h4>
<p>A <strong>case study</strong> is an argument with evidence, in six parts. (1) Context and role: the product, the users, the team, the timeline and exactly what <em>you</em> did. (2) The problem, with evidence: research findings and baseline metrics. (3) Process: the methods you chose and why — not a tour of every method you know. (4) Two or three key decisions, with the alternatives you rejected and the trade-offs. (5) Outcome, measured: SUS 58 → 79, median task time 4:10 → 2:05, WCAG 2.2 AA with zero axe violations and a clean screen-reader pass. (6) Reflection: what you would do differently. Show artifacts — sketches, flows, a before and after — with captions that carry the argument. Three deep case studies beat ten thumbnails, because hiring managers skim for minutes, not hours. Be honest about team contributions, anonymize work under NDA, and label concept projects as concept projects. Make the portfolio site itself accessible: a portfolio that fails WCAG fails the interview.</p>
<h4>Working with product and engineering</h4>
<p>Good critique starts with the goal and the constraints, asks for feedback against them, and separates critique ("this label will be misread because…") from direction ("make it blue"); "I like it" is not feedback. Formats such as rose–thorn–bud keep sessions moving. Design reviews happen at milestones, and design QA before release compares the build with the design, token by token. Dual-track agile runs discovery and delivery side by side inside Domain 20's sprints. DesignOps is the operations layer: tooling, design-system governance, hiring and rituals, and ResearchOps (participant panels, consent templates, a research repository such as Dovetail). Shift accessibility left: acceptance criteria in tickets, linting (eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y), axe in continuous integration through Playwright and @axe-core/playwright, a manual screen-reader pass before release, and an Accessibility Conformance Report updated each release. The <strong>handoff</strong> runs both ways: engineers return constraints, data shapes and edge cases early enough to change the design.</p>
<h4>Content design and microcopy</h4>
<p>Content design (Sarah Winters, from her work on GOV.UK) starts from the user's need and writes what people need, in the words they use, in the order they need it. <strong>Microcopy</strong> is the small text that makes interfaces work: button labels, errors, empty states, hints, confirmations. Rules that survive testing: buttons are verbs that say what happens ("Save as-built", not "OK"); front-load keywords; use plain language (the US Plain Writing Act of 2010 requires it of federal agencies; aim for about an eighth-grade reading level for public content); keep one term per concept, and keep a glossary; write errors as Module 3 taught; turn empty states into instructions ("No cards due. Next review tomorrow at 6:00 a.m."); never blame the user. Voice is the constant personality; tone adapts — calm in errors, brief in success. Test words like any design: highlighter tests, cloze tests, five-second tests, A/B tests on labels.</p>
<h4>The Google UX Design certificate, project by project</h4>
<p>As of October 2026, the Google UX Design Professional Certificate on Coursera is eight courses: Foundations of User Experience (UX) Design; Start the UX Design Process: Empathize, Define, and Ideate; Build Wireframes and Low-Fidelity Prototypes; Conduct UX Research and Test Early Concepts; Create High-Fidelity Designs and Prototypes in Figma; Build Dynamic User Interfaces (UI) for Websites; Design a User Experience for Social Good &amp; Prepare for Jobs; and a bonus, Accelerate Your Job Search with AI. Coursera estimates six months at ten hours a week, charges $49 a month in the US and Canada after a seven-day trial (most learners finish for under $300, and it is included in Coursera Plus), and the program carries an ACE recommendation of up to 10 college credits. The portfolio grows in sequence: <em>Project 1</em>, a mobile app, carried through Courses 2–5 (empathize and define, wireframes and paper prototypes, a usability study, then high-fidelity mockups and a Figma prototype); <em>Project 2</em>, a responsive website (Course 6: information architecture, sitemaps, layouts); <em>Project 3</em>, a cross-platform social-good experience, a dedicated mobile app plus a responsive website (Course 7), followed by portfolio and interview preparation. Strategy: run every project on a real problem you can reach — crews, this app, a local organization — and add this domain's research rigor, statistics and accessibility audit. That is what separates your three projects from thousands built on the same prompts.</p>
<h4>CPACC, then WAS</h4>
<p>IAAP (the International Association of Accessibility Professionals, a division of G3ict) runs six exam sessions a year, each with a four-week testing window and fixed application, payment and scheduling deadlines; exams are delivered by Pearson VUE online (OnVUE) or at test centers, scored on a 200–800 scale with 600 to pass, and results arrive four to six weeks after the session closes. CPACC is 100 multiple-choice questions in two hours; IAAP's April 2025 candidate handbook lists $410 for members, $510 for non-members and $170 for candidates in emerging and developing economies, with reduced retake fees within a year. It asks for about a year of accessibility-related work or a role with accessibility responsibility, and lets you describe your background if you have less. WAS is 75 questions in two hours ($455 member, $555 non-member), screened for three to five years of hands-on technical accessibility work. Passing both earns the CPWA. Certifications last three years, renewed with continuing-education credits (45 for CPACC, 35 for WAS). Sequence: CPACC once this domain and some real audits are behind you; WAS after a year of remediation work. In US federal contracting, DHS's free Section 508 Trusted Tester certification is a useful companion.</p>
<h4>Career paths and pay</h4>
<table><tr><th>Path</th><th>Core work</th><th>Proof that gets you hired</th></tr>
<tr><td>UX / product designer</td><td>Flows, prototypes, visual systems</td><td>Three case studies with outcomes</td></tr>
<tr><td>UX researcher</td><td>Studies, synthesis, measurement</td><td>Research plans, reports, statistics</td></tr>
<tr><td>Content designer / UX writer</td><td>Words, structure, microcopy</td><td>Before-and-after content with test data</td></tr>
<tr><td>UX engineer / design technologist</td><td>Coded components, design systems</td><td>A component library with tokens and tests</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accessibility tester → engineer</td><td>Audits, remediation, AT testing</td><td>CPACC, then WAS; published audits</td></tr>
<tr><td>Usable security</td><td>Login, warnings, phishing-resistant flows</td><td>Security certifications plus UX research</td></tr></table>
<p>On pay, read every number with its source. Google's certificate page cites Lightcast job-posting data for 2025: over 63,000 open UX design jobs in the US and a median entry-level salary of $125,000 — posting data skews toward large-metro and big-tech roles, so treat it as a ceiling, not a forecast. The Bureau of Labor Statistics groups most UX and UI designers under web and digital interface designers, with a national median near $98,000 in its May 2024 data. Expect a career changer's first offer well below the posting median, in a regional market below the national one; check BLS data for your metro area and current postings before you negotiate (Domain 26).</p>
<h4>How this maps to the certifications</h4>
<p>Google's Course 7 builds the portfolio and interview skills and Course 8 the AI-assisted job search. CPACC's third domain (Standards, Laws and Management Strategies, 20%) covers governance, procurement and integrating accessibility across an organization — this module's process material; WAS's remediation domain covers prioritizing and recommending fixes, exactly what a case study's "decisions" section shows.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>One case study, outlined.</strong> Headline: "Halving as-built logging time for 40 fiber crews." Role: sole designer and front-end developer, 10 weeks, reviewed by a crew lead. Problem: 1 entry in 12 needed correction; median 4 min 10 s per entry; SUS 58. Process: 6 contextual inquiries, a 120-person survey (±8.9 points), an open card sort with 15 people, three test rounds of five. Decisions: barcode scan for reel IDs (rejected voice entry — noisy sites, gloves on); one flat splice-type list (Hick's arithmetic in Module 3); offline-first saving with a visible sync state. Outcome: median 2 min 05 s, corrections 1 in 40, SUS 79, WCAG 2.2 AA with zero axe violations and NVDA and TalkBack passes. Reflection: recruit night-shift crews earlier. Exam plan alongside it: finish this domain and the Google certificate in months 1–6; apply for the IAAP session that tests in month 5 and sit CPACC; target WAS in year two after remediation work; list both under "Accessibility" on the résumé, next to the security certifications.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"A portfolio is a gallery of pretty screens.\" Hiring managers look for problem framing, evidence, decisions and measured outcomes; screens illustrate the story.","\"The Google certificate gets you hired by itself.\" It teaches a process and yields three projects that look like everyone else's; real research, accessibility depth and measured results differentiate you.","\"CPACC is a technical WCAG coding exam.\" CPACC is foundational and broad (disability, universal design, law, management); WAS is the technical web exam."],
problems:[
{q:"Rewrite the case-study headline 'Redesigned an app' as an outcome headline.", a:"'Cut as-built logging time in half for 40 fiber crews (SUS 58 → 79, WCAG 2.2 AA).'"},
{q:"Rewrite three pieces of microcopy: a 'Submit' button, the error 'Something went wrong', and the empty state 'No data'.", a:"'Save as-built'; 'We couldn't upload your entry. It's saved on this device and will upload when you're back online.'; 'No reels logged today. Scan a reel label to start.'"},
{q:"A job you want requires CPACC in five months. IAAP runs six sessions a year with four-week windows and results 4–6 weeks after a session closes. Plan backward.", a:"Results must exist by month 5, so the session must close by about month 3.5; apply before that session's application deadline (month 1–2), study through Module 5, sit early in the window, and keep the following session as the retake fallback."},
{q:"Choose a career path for someone with field-telecom, engineering and security training, and justify it with two pieces of evidence.", a:"Accessibility engineer (or usable-security researcher): audits and remediation reuse the pentest reporting craft; field research shows the ability to study users in hard contexts; CPACC → WAS gives employer-recognized proof."},
{q:"Write two accessibility acceptance criteria for a sprint ticket that adds a modal dialog.", a:"(1) On open, focus moves into the dialog, Tab cycles within it, Esc closes it and focus returns to the trigger; (2) the dialog has an accessible name via aria-labelledby, and axe in CI reports zero violations for the open state."}
],
checks:[{q:"The three Google certificate portfolio projects?",a:"A mobile app, a responsive website, and a cross-platform (app plus responsive site) social-good experience."},{q:"CPACC format and passing score?",a:"100 multiple-choice questions in 2 hours; scaled 200–800, 600 to pass."},{q:"What earns the CPWA?",a:"Passing both CPACC and WAS."}],
explain:"Pitch to ADA, in two minutes, one portfolio case study from this domain — problem, evidence, decisions, outcome — then explain why you will take CPACC before WAS."}
],
listening:[],
labs:[
{id:"p44lab1", title:"Usability test of this app: five tasks and SUS", goal:"Measure this learning app's usability with real people and numbers.", steps:["Write a test plan: research questions, five realistic tasks without interface words (e.g., open Domain 44 Module 3 and its simple version; read a key term's definition using only the keyboard; answer a quiz question and tell whether you were right; review five Recall cards; find the CPACC fee), success criteria for each, and a consent form.","Recruit five participants (friends or coworkers are fine; include one who uses a keyboard or screen reader if you can), get written informed consent, and pilot the script on yourself.","Run 30-minute moderated think-aloud sessions; record success, time on task and a Single Ease Question after each task; give the SUS at the end.","Score each SUS, compute the mean with a 95% confidence interval, and the adjusted-Wald interval for each task's success rate.","Write severity-rated findings with evidence and acceptance criteria, and rank the top three fixes."], deliverable:"Test plan, consent form, raw data sheet, SUS and task statistics with intervals, and a findings report of at least five issues."},
{id:"p44lab2", title:"A research plan and three interviews", goal:"Discover a real need in a context you can reach.", steps:["Pick a workflow you can access with permission (end-of-shift as-built logging, a hobby group's scheduling, a family member's banking app).","Write the plan: background, three research questions, method, screener, consent, schedule, analysis and deliverables.","Write a semi-structured guide of open, past-behavior prompts; remove every leading or hypothetical question.","Run three interviews (one contextual, at the place of work if allowed); take notes and record only with consent.","Affinity-map the notes; write three job stories and one journey map; list two assumptions the interviews disproved."], deliverable:"Research plan, guide, anonymized notes, affinity map photo, job stories and journey map."},
{id:"p44lab3", title:"Card sort and tree test", goal:"Build and verify an information architecture from evidence.", steps:["Write 30–40 cards from this app's content and tools (phases, labs, Recall, ADA, certification pages, listening).","Run an open card sort with as many participants as you can (aim for 15) using OptimalSort's free tier or paper cards.","Build the similarity matrix and read the dendrogram; draft a hierarchy and labels.","Tree-test the hierarchy with eight tasks and 20 or more participants; record success, directness and first click.","Revise the labels or structure where success falls below 80%, and note the sample-size limitation honestly."], deliverable:"Card list, similarity matrix, proposed hierarchy, tree-test results table and a one-page recommendation."},
{id:"p44lab4", title:"Figma: wireframe to prototype with a token sheet", goal:"Redesign one screen from sketch to clickable, token-driven prototype.", steps:["Redesign this app's quiz feedback and key-term popover: three paper sketches, then a grayscale wireframe in Figma.","Build components with variants (default, hover, focus, correct, incorrect, disabled) and auto layout; mark focus states visibly.","Create variables for color, spacing and type with light and dark modes; replace every raw value with a variable.","Prototype the flow (answer → feedback → explanation → next) and test it with two people.","Export a token sheet in Design Tokens Community Group JSON and write the CSS custom properties it compiles to."], deliverable:"Figma file link or exports, the prototype, a token JSON file, the compiled CSS, and notes from the two tests."},
{id:"p44lab5", title:"Accessibility audit of your own web project to WCAG 2.2 AA", goal:"Find, fix and verify real barriers in code you own.", steps:["Choose your Domain 5 site (or another project you own) and define the scope: five representative pages or states.","Run axe (npx @axe-core/cli with WCAG 2.2 AA tags), Lighthouse's accessibility category and WAVE; log every violation with its criterion.","Do the manual passes: keyboard only, NVDA with Firefox or VoiceOver with Safari, TalkBack on a phone, 200% zoom and 320 px reflow, forced text spacing.","Fix every Level A and AA failure, starting with semantic HTML before any ARIA; commit each fix separately.","Retest, add axe to your test suite so regressions fail the build, and write a one-page conformance summary."], deliverable:"Audit log (before and after), commits, the screen-reader notes, and the conformance summary."},
{id:"p44lab6", title:"Contrast and typography pass", goal:"Make a palette and type system you can defend with numbers.", steps:["Write a small script (Domain 4 Python or JavaScript) that computes WCAG contrast ratios for every text and background pair in your palette.","Fix every pair below 4.5:1 for text and 3:1 for components, keeping the hue; record before and after.","Define a type scale (ratio of your choice), line heights and a maximum line length in ch.","Check the result in grayscale and with protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia emulation; remove any meaning carried by color alone.","Apply the tokens to one page of your project and verify 200% zoom and forced text spacing."], deliverable:"The script, a contrast table, the type scale, emulation screenshots and the updated page."},
{id:"p44lab7", title:"Portfolio case study", goal:"Turn this domain's labs into one hireable story.", steps:["Pick the lab with the strongest measured outcome as the spine of the story.","Draft the six parts: context and role, problem with evidence, process, decisions, outcome, reflection.","Choose five artifacts and write captions that carry the argument.","Publish it on an accessible page (semantic headings, alt text, contrast) and run your Lab 5 audit on it.","Get a critique from two people against the case study's goals and revise once."], deliverable:"A published case study page and the critique notes with the changes you made."}
],
capstone:{title:"Redesign one real workflow, end to end and accessibly", brief:"Choose a workflow you have permission to study and change — field as-built logging, this app's Recall deck, or your Domain 5 site. Research it (plan, three interviews or contextual inquiries, consent), verify its structure (card sort or tree test), redesign it in Figma with components, variants and a token sheet, implement the change in code, test it with five users before and after (tasks, SUS with confidence intervals), audit it to WCAG 2.2 AA with axe and a screen reader, and publish the whole story as a portfolio case study.", rubric:["Research is ethical and rigorous: consent, non-leading guide, affinity synthesis, job stories grounded in quotes.","Design decisions cite evidence and arithmetic (Fitts's or Hick's law, contrast ratios, funnel or test data).","Implementation uses design tokens and semantic HTML; WCAG 2.2 AA verified by tools and manual keyboard and screen-reader passes.","Before-and-after measurement: task success, time and SUS with intervals, interpreted honestly.","The case study is clear, accessible and reproducible by ADA from its artifacts."]},
exam:[
{id:"p44q1", type:"mc", q:"ISO 9241-11 defines usability in terms of:", options:["Speed, beauty and reliability","Effectiveness, efficiency and satisfaction for specified users, goals and context of use","Conversion rate and retention","Contrast, typography and layout"], answer:1, why:"The 2018 definition names effectiveness, efficiency and satisfaction, always relative to specified users, goals and a context of use."},
{id:"p44q2", type:"mc", q:"The Double Diamond's four phases, in order:", options:["Empathize, Define, Ideate, Test","Discover, Define, Develop, Deliver","Plan, Build, Measure, Learn","Research, Design, Code, Ship"], answer:1, why:"Design Council: diverge and converge on the problem (Discover, Define), then on the solution (Develop, Deliver)."},
{id:"p44q3", type:"mc", q:"A user intends to tap Save but hits the adjacent Delete. Norman classifies this as:", options:["A mistake","A slip","A gulf of evaluation","A violation"], answer:1, why:"Right goal, wrong action: a slip, prevented by spacing, constraints and undo."},
{id:"p44q4", type:"mc", q:"At D = 600 px, widening a target from 24 to 48 px changes Fitts's index of difficulty from 4.70 bits to about:", options:["2.35 bits","3.75 bits","4.20 bits","9.40 bits"], answer:1, why:"log2(600/48 + 1) = log2(13.5) ≈ 3.75 bits."},
{id:"p44q5", type:"mc", q:"Hick's law describes decision time best for:", options:["Scanning an unfamiliar long list","Choosing among familiar, equally likely options","Typing speed","Remembering seven items"], answer:1, why:"RT = a + b·log2(n + 1) holds for known, equally likely choices; unfamiliar lists are scanned roughly linearly."},
{id:"p44q6", type:"mc", q:"A survey of 400 people (worst case p = 0.5) has a 95% margin of error of about:", options:["±1 point","±2.5 points","±4.9 points","±10 points"], answer:2, why:"1.96 × √(0.25/400) = 0.049 — and it says nothing about sampling bias."},
{id:"p44q7", type:"mc", q:"Which pair is correct?", options:["Card sort evaluates a finished hierarchy; tree test generates categories","Card sort reveals how people group content; tree test checks whether a proposed hierarchy is findable","Both measure visual hierarchy","Both require eye tracking"], answer:1, why:"Card sorting is generative for structure; tree testing is evaluative for findability."},
{id:"p44q8", type:"mc", q:"#767676 text on a white background measures 4.54:1. For WCAG AA it:", options:["Fails for all text","Passes normal and large text","Passes large text only","Passes only at AAA"], answer:1, why:"AA requires 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text; 4.54:1 clears both (AAA would need 7:1)."},
{id:"p44q9", type:"mc", q:"WCAG 2.2's Target Size (Minimum), Level AA, requires:", options:["44 × 44 CSS px","48 × 48 dp","24 × 24 CSS px or sufficient spacing","16 × 16 CSS px"], answer:2, why:"2.5.8 sets 24 × 24 CSS px with a spacing exception; 44 × 44 is the AAA criterion 2.5.5."},
{id:"p44q10", type:"mc", q:"The first rule of ARIA says:", options:["Put role attributes on every element","Use a native HTML element or attribute with the needed semantics and behavior instead of adding ARIA","Always use aria-label instead of visible text","ARIA replaces keyboard support"], answer:1, why:"Native elements bring role, name, focus and keyboard behavior for free; ARIA only changes semantics."},
{id:"p44q11", type:"mc", q:"What is the accessible name of a button with aria-label='Close dialog' that contains an icon marked aria-hidden='true'?", options:["Nothing","'button'","'Close dialog'","The icon's file name"], answer:2, why:"aria-label supplies the name; the hidden icon contributes nothing."},
{id:"p44q12", type:"mc", q:"Under the DOJ's April 2026 interim final rule, a US county of 80,000 people must meet WCAG 2.1 AA by:", options:["24 April 2026","26 April 2027","26 April 2028","It is exempt"], answer:1, why:"Entities serving 50,000 or more moved from 24 April 2026 to 26 April 2027; smaller entities to 26 April 2028."},
{id:"p44q13", type:"mc", q:"The European Accessibility Act has applied to newly marketed products and services since:", options:["23 September 2020","28 June 2022","28 June 2025","28 June 2030"], answer:2, why:"Directive (EU) 2019/882: transposition by 28 June 2022, application from 28 June 2025, with transition to 2030 for existing contracts and products in use."},
{id:"p44q14", type:"mc", q:"SUS responses 4,2,5,1,4,2,5,2,4,2 score:", options:["68","75","82.5","90"], answer:2, why:"Odd items give 17, even items 16; (17 + 16) × 2.5 = 82.5."},
{id:"p44q15", type:"mc", q:"If the average problem affects 31% of users, five test users find about what share of problems?", options:["31%","50%","84%","100%"], answer:2, why:"1 − (1 − 0.31)^5 = 1 − 0.156 ≈ 84%, for one homogeneous user group."},
{id:"p44q16", type:"mc", q:"A site scores 100 in Lighthouse's accessibility category. The correct conclusion:", options:["It conforms to WCAG 2.2 AA","Automated checks passed; manual keyboard and screen-reader testing are still required","It no longer needs captions","It meets Section 508 automatically"], answer:1, why:"Automated rules detect roughly 40–57% of barriers; conformance needs manual testing."},
{id:"p44q17", type:"sa", q:"A form shows 'Invalid input' in red under a postcode field when the user types 'SW1A 1AA'. Rewrite the behavior and message, and name three WCAG criteria your fix satisfies.", answer:"Accept the space (normalize it on the server) so the error never appears; where a real error exists, show text such as 'Enter a postcode like SW1A 1AA' next to the field, linked with aria-describedby and aria-invalid, with an icon so meaning is not color alone, plus a focused error summary on submit. Satisfies 3.3.1 Error Identification, 3.3.3 Error Suggestion and 1.4.1 Use of Color (and 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions).", rubric:["Prevents the error by accepting the format","Specific, plain-language message tied to the field","Not color alone; programmatically associated","Three correct criteria named"]},
{id:"p44q18", type:"sa", q:"Design a five-participant moderated usability test for 'review today's due flashcards' and explain how you will report SUS and task success honestly.", answer:"Recruit five target learners with informed consent; one realistic task with written success criteria and no interface words; think-aloud with neutral prompts; record success, time and SEQ; SUS at the end. Report the SUS mean with a 95% t-interval and task success with an adjusted-Wald interval (4 of 5 is about 36–98%), state that five users find common problems but do not measure rates precisely, and list severity-rated findings with fixes.", rubric:["Consent and realistic task with success criteria","Think-aloud without leading","SUS and success reported with intervals","States the limits of n = 5 and turns findings into fixes"]}
],
cards:[
{id:"p44c1", front:"Usability", back:"ISO 9241-11:2018: the extent to which specified users can achieve specified goals with effectiveness, efficiency and satisfaction in a specified context of use."},
{id:"p44c2", front:"Double Diamond", back:"Design Council model (2004–05): Discover and Define the right problem, then Develop and Deliver the right solution — two cycles of divergence and convergence."},
{id:"p44c3", front:"Contextual inquiry", back:"Field research where the work happens, in a master–apprentice stance: watch, ask while they work, and confirm interpretations on the spot (Beyer & Holtzblatt)."},
{id:"p44c4", front:"Jobs to be done", back:"The progress a person 'hires' a product to make in a circumstance; written as a job story: When [situation], I want to [motivation], so I can [outcome]."},
{id:"p44c5", front:"Card sort", back:"Participants group content cards into categories (open, closed or hybrid); analyzed with a similarity matrix and dendrogram to build information architecture."},
{id:"p44c6", front:"Tree testing", back:"Evaluating a text-only hierarchy with find-it tasks; measures success, directness, time and first click before any visual design."},
{id:"p44c7", front:"Affordance", back:"The relationship between an object's properties and a person's capabilities that determines what actions are possible (a button affords pressing)."},
{id:"p44c8", front:"Signifier", back:"Any perceivable cue that shows where and how to act — a label, an underline, a raised edge (Norman, 2013); designers design signifiers."},
{id:"p44c9", front:"Fitts's law", back:"Pointing time MT = a + b·log2(D/W + 1): bigger, closer targets are faster; the basis for touch-target minimums."},
{id:"p44c10", front:"Hick's law", back:"Choice time RT = a + b·log2(n + 1) for familiar, equally likely options; does not apply to scanning unfamiliar lists."},
{id:"p44c11", front:"Design token", back:"A named design decision stored as data (primitive → semantic → component), compiled to CSS custom properties, Android and iOS values."},
{id:"p44c12", front:"Contrast ratio", back:"(L1 + 0.05)/(L2 + 0.05) from relative luminance; WCAG AA needs 4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text and UI components."},
{id:"p44c13", front:"WCAG", back:"W3C Web Content Accessibility Guidelines; 2.2 (2023, ISO/IEC 40500:2025) has 86 success criteria at A, AA and AAA; AA is the usual legal target."},
{id:"p44c14", front:"POUR", back:"WCAG's four principles: Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, Robust."},
{id:"p44c15", front:"Accessible name", back:"The name assistive technology announces for an element, computed from aria-labelledby, aria-label, native labels or content, with title and placeholder as last resorts."},
{id:"p44c16", front:"System Usability Scale", back:"Brooke's ten-item questionnaire: odd items −1, even items 5 − response, sum × 2.5 = 0–100; the average is about 68; not a percentage."},
{id:"p44c17", front:"Heuristic evaluation", back:"Three to five evaluators inspect an interface against Nielsen's ten heuristics and rate severity 0–4; cheap and fast, never a substitute for user testing."},
{id:"p44c18", front:"Microcopy", back:"The small interface text — button labels, errors, hints, empty states — that tells people what to do and what happened."}
]
});
